WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:18.690
Imagine that this is the beginning of the
last thing you’ll ever see, an empty landscape

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.637
with thin lines scratched across it.

00:00:21.637 --> 00:00:27.386
But those lines suddenly sharpen and gather
into a dense mass that spreads from the crown

00:00:27.386 --> 00:00:32.358
that sits atop a giant, studded with greens
and yellows.

00:00:32.358 --> 00:00:36.349
A giant that is in search of one thing: food.

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:42.565
And then imagine, that you are that food.

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:49.859
Tiny in the face of this alien whose body
is designed to take you in and break you down.

00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:56.171
And as it retracts, pulling away from the
scenery, you disappear with it.

00:00:56.171 --> 00:01:00.300
We're letting magnification and music do
a lot of the heavy lifting when it comes to

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.659
setting the scene.

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:10.039
Up close, this animal is upsetting, its movements
slow until they are sudden, peaceful until

00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:11.869
they are predatory.

00:01:11.869 --> 00:01:15.060
But from further away, things are a little
different.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.080
It still looks strange, but it seems less
overwhelming than before.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.760
It seems just kind of chill, even as this
other large organism brushes by.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.770
The animal we’ve been focused on belongs
to the genus Collotheca, making it one of

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.277
our favorite types of animals: a rotifer.

00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:37.976
And we’ve happened upon many rotifer mishaps
and adventures in the microcosmos before.

00:01:37.976 --> 00:01:44.250
They’re always being bullied or eaten or caught 
up in some tragic tale of their own making.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.137
But in the case of the Collotheca, their biggest
bullies might be themselves.

00:01:49.137 --> 00:01:55.044
Because these organisms love to eat, even
if that means eating each other.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.960
Collotheca are put in an awkward position
when it comes to seeking out food because

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.597
they can’t easily hunt it down.

00:02:06.597 --> 00:02:10.960
Most Collotheca species are sessile, which
means they don’t really swim around.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.861
Instead, they spend most of their lives stuck,
attaching themselves to whatever is in their

00:02:15.861 --> 00:02:20.490
vicinity and seems like it might be a good
home, like the ones you see here.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:25.670
And while they’re there, the Collotheca
surround themselves in a gelatinous tube,

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:31.414
giving them a sort of squishy sleeping bag they 
can retract into when something disturbs them.

00:02:31.414 --> 00:02:34.127
And it turns out, it’s very easy to disturb
them.

00:02:34.127 --> 00:02:38.926
Those long threads we saw at the beginning
of the video aren’t just decoration.

00:02:38.926 --> 00:02:43.430
They are there to help the rotifer sense
the world around them, both so they can recognize

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.730
danger and so that they can find food.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.280
The hairs are called cilia, and we’ve seen
them on other rotifers before.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:56.250
In fact, they’re a defining feature, part
of the inspiration for the name “rotifer,”

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.267
which translates to “wheel bearer”.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:04.470
It’s the fast spinning whirling motion of
the cilia that gives the head of the rotifer

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.580
its wheel-y appearance.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.610
But while the cilia are notable on all rotifer,
they are particularly long and noticeable

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.255
on the Collotheca.

00:03:13.255 --> 00:03:15.813
And they also seem to be extremely sensitive.

00:03:15.813 --> 00:03:20.350
James, our master of microscopes, has found
that if he’s recording Collotheca, he has

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:25.388
to be extra careful to not walk around the
microscope.

00:03:25.388 --> 00:03:31.310
If he does walk, the vibrations in his steps
reach the Collotheca and drive them to retract

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.108
into their gelatinous safety vest.

00:03:34.108 --> 00:03:38.740
Like other rotifers, Collotheca appear to
be able to use their cilia to create currents

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.970
that draw food into their mouths.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:46.780
Whether it’s algae or some other kind of
single-celled organism, or even a tiny crustacean

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:52.879
like a water flea, the Collotheca probably
doesn’t care as long as it gets fed.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:58.600
It uses its enormous mouth to close in on
whatever it’s eating, wrapping it up in

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:04.860
the bony jaw called the trophi, and then swallowing
its prey in a jerky motion.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.890
Based on what James has observed, he’s been
starting to suspect something kind of funny

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.920
about how Collotheca get their food because
sometimes, you’ll see smaller organisms

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.670
just swim right into their mouths.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:23.010
So James thinks they may be getting a little
extra help, that when the Collotheca retracts

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:30.136
into itself, it’s actually pushing a little bit of 
the stuff in its stomach into its giant mouth.

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:36.710
And in that regurgitated mass are nutrients
that could be attracting single-celled organisms

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.838
toward the rotifer.

00:04:38.838 --> 00:04:43.720
Now we’re not sure if that’s what
happening—that the rotifer is puking into

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.600
own mouth to lure in more food.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:52.366
But if it is true, it's still not the weirdest
way that Collotheca gets a meal.

00:04:52.366 --> 00:04:55.680
That would probably be the cannibalism.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.316
But not just any form of cannibalism will
do for the Collotheca.

00:04:59.316 --> 00:05:04.712
It is specifically, a baby cannibal, fully 
willing to eat its own child.

00:05:06.793 --> 00:05:11.780
Cannibalism is a striking behavior for Collotheca,
but not necessarily a surprising one.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.490
In the same study where this behavior was
documented, another non-Collotheca rotifer

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:21.650
was observed engaging in sexual cannibalism,
where the female rotifer would eat the male

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.430
rotifer after mating was completed.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:29.590
That behavior is not unheard of in the animal
kingdom, where insects and spiders turn their

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.577
last mate into their next meal.

00:05:32.577 --> 00:05:36.789
It’s not clear if Collotheca do the same
thing to their male counterparts.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:42.417
In fact, for a long time, it wasn’t even
clear that they had male counterparts.

00:05:42.417 --> 00:05:47.180
We’ve known about Collotheca for more than
a century, but the first male Collotheca wasn’t

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.350
observed until 1986.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:54.960
And it turns out that male Collotheca were
generally smaller, and most importantly, their

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.301
lifespans were super short—on the order
of 20 hours.

00:05:59.301 --> 00:06:05.103
It is as if they were born just to breed
and then die soon after.

00:06:05.103 --> 00:06:08.620
But we're getting away from our original
topic: the cannibalism.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:13.099
The captions from the paper documenting this
tell the story quite succinctly.

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:19.120
From Panel A: “female neonate emerging from
gelatinous tube.”

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:25.360
From Panels B-D: “adult female ingesting
female neonate.”

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:31.099
It’s simple, poignant, and tragic for the
female neonate.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:37.180
It’s also completely unclear to us how common
this behavior is, and why the rotifer mother

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:38.807
might engage in it.

00:06:38.807 --> 00:06:42.247
It does not seem to be a great way to
pass on your genes.

00:06:42.247 --> 00:06:47.050
Perhaps it was just in need of a nutritious
snack, perhaps it was a mistake.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:51.998
Perhaps, in the microcosmos, rotifers can't
be choosers.

00:06:56.179 --> 00:07:01.927
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:01.927 --> 00:07:06.740
And thank you especially to these people here
on the screen right now, they are our patrons

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:07.628
on Patreon.

00:07:07.628 --> 00:07:14.237
They make it possible for us to continue to
explore this bizarre and beautiful world,

00:07:14.237 --> 00:07:19.420
and to make observations that we can't yet
confirm but hopefully some day we will be

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:20.420
able to.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:24.806
Like that rotifers, maybe, puke in their own
mouths to attract food.

00:07:24.806 --> 00:07:29.520
If you like what we do here you can go to
Patreon.com/JournytoMicro.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.830
If you want to see more from our master of
microscopes, James Weiss, you can check out

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:36.020
Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram or TikTok.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:40.643
And if you want to see more from us here, there's 
